<BaseLayout title="Source Policy & Evidence Types" description="How HomeGeneratorGuide distinguishes manufacturer specifications, government guidance, local requirements, editorial interpretation and planning estimates."><article class="editorial-container publication-section prose-hgg"><h1>Source policy & evidence types</h1><p>Updated October 5, 2026. Our aim is to make every important conclusion traceable without giving uncertain information a false appearance of precision.</p><div class="planner-actions">{labels.map(type=><FactTypeBadge type={type}/>)}</div><h2>Use the source that fits the claim</h2><p>Exact-model facts should come from the manufacturer’s exact product page, specification, installation or owner’s manual, warranty or accessory documentation. Government and safety sources support their own guidance. A local authority supports only its stated jurisdiction and scope. Industry guidance and editorial interpretation are identified separately.</p><h2>Preserve conditions and disagreements</h2><p>A sound number needs its operating mode, distance and other stated test conditions. Consumption is tied to fuel, units and load percentage. An ATS package is a distinct configuration. A newer source does not automatically erase a conflicting specification: unresolved values are withheld or shown with an explicit conflict note.</p><h2>Unknown is a useful result</h2><p>A missing field means the evidence has not established it. It does not mean that the feature is absent. We do not fill a model’s blanks from a neighboring model, dealer copy or a brand-wide assumption.</p><h2>Dates describe the work done</h2><p>Source-check dates record when the linked material was examined; they are not manufacturer revision dates or professional approval dates. Original publication dates that were not retained remain unrecorded. A page edit does not imply every old source was reverified.</p><h2>Professional review is a separate event</h2><p>Technical professional review pending. No licensed reviewer is currently recorded. Future review records require identity, credential evidence, jurisdiction, scope, date and the reviewed version. See the <a href="/review-process/">review process</a> and <a href="/corrections-policy/">correction policy</a>.</p><h2>Funding and corrections</h2><p>Read our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">funding disclosure</a>. Send a source-specific correction through <a href="/contact/">contact</a>; include the page, claim and supporting document. Manufacturer documentation is evidence of a published specification, not independent reliability testing.</p></article></BaseLayout>
